        <p>The student visa covers language schools, universities, and accredited vocational programmes.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="display: grid; gap: 1rem;">
        <div style="padding: 1.3rem 1.5rem; background: var(--white); border-radius: 10px; border-left: 4px solid var(--red);">
          <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Language Schools (most common for Australians)</h3>
          <p style="margin: 0.5rem 0 0; font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--muted);">Intensive Spanish courses at accredited language schools. Barcelona, Valencia, and Seville are top choices. Look for schools accredited by Instituto Cervantes. Programmes typically run 3–12 months and qualify for a student visa.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding: 1.3rem 1.5rem; background: var(--white); border-radius: 10px; border-left: 4px solid var(--navy);">
          <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">University Exchange Programmes</h3>
          <p style="margin: 0.5rem 0 0; font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--muted);">Many Australian universities have exchange agreements with Spanish institutions. A semester or year at a Spanish university is a popular option. Your Australian university's international office can often assist with documentation.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding: 1.3rem 1.5rem; background: var(--white); border-radius: 10px; border-left: 4px solid #e67e22;">
          <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Full Degree Programmes</h3>
          <p style="margin: 0.5rem 0 0; font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--muted);">Australians can enrol directly at Spanish universities for Bachelor's, Master's, or PhD degrees. Architecture, design, fine arts, and medicine are particularly popular. Tuition is often significantly lower than Australian university fees.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding: 1.3rem 1.5rem; background: var(--white); border-radius: 10px; border-left: 4px solid #3399ff;">
          <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Vocational &amp; Professional Courses</h3>
          <p style="margin: 0.5rem 0 0; font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--muted);">Culinary arts, flamenco, photography, film, and design programmes. Spain has a rich vocational education scene. If the programme is officially recognised and lasts 90+ days, it qualifies.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Financial Requirements ──────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Financial Requirements for Australian Applicants</h2>
        <p>What the Spanish consulate looks for — and what does not count.</p>
      </div>

      <p>Spain requires evidence of approximately <strong>€600 per month</strong> for the duration of your stay (around €7,200 for a full year). Australian bank statements in AUD are accepted — the consulate assesses the equivalent value at the time of application.</p>

      <div style="background: var(--cream); border-radius: 12px; padding: 2rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <h3 style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 1rem;">What Counts — and What Doesn't</h3>
        <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 1rem;">
          <div style="padding: 1rem; background: #f0fdf4; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #86efac;">
            <h3 style="color: #166534; font-size: 0.9rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Accepted</h3>
            <ul style="margin: 0;">
              <li>Australian bank savings accounts (liquid)</li>
              <li>Offset accounts with accessible funds</li>
              <li>Term deposits if breakable without major penalty</li>
              <li>Parent/sponsor statements + support letter</li>
              <li>Scholarship or funding letters</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div style="padding: 1rem; background: #fef2f2; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #fca5a5;">
            <h3 style="color: #991b1b; font-size: 0.9rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Not Accepted</h3>
            <ul style="margin: 0;">
              <li>Superannuation (locked retirement savings)</li>
              <li>Salary income only (no savings to show)</li>
              <li>Property equity (non-liquid)</li>
              <li>Investment accounts with restricted access</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--warn">
        <div class="msv__alert-icon">⚠</div>
        <p><strong>Superannuation cannot be used as proof of funds.</strong> This is a common misconception among Australian applicants. The Spanish consulate requires accessible liquid funds — superannuation is locked until preservation age and does not qualify.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Documents Checklist ──────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section msv__section--cream">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Documents Checklist for Australian Applicants</h2>
        <p>Australia-specific documents plus the standard student visa requirements.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 2rem; padding: 1.5rem; background: var(--white); border-radius: 12px; border-left: 4px solid var(--red);">
        <h3>AFP National Police Check</h3>
        <p style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">All Australian applicants must provide a <strong>National Police Check</strong> issued by the Australian Federal Police (AFP). This must be apostilled through the Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade (DFAT) before submission.</p>
        <ul style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
          <li>Apply at <a href="https://www.afp.gov.au/national-police-checks" target="_blank">afp.gov.au</a> or through an AFP-accredited agency</li>
          <li>Standard processing: approximately 10–15 business days</li>
          <li>Expedited service available (additional fee)</li>
          <li>The result must be less than 3 months old at the time of consulate submission</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 2rem; padding: 1.5rem; background: var(--white); border-radius: 12px; border-left: 4px solid var(--navy);">
        <h3>DFAT Apostille</h3>
        <p style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">Australia is a signatory to the Hague Convention. Your AFP certificate must be apostilled by the <strong>Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade (DFAT)</strong> before it is accepted by the Spanish consulate.</p>
        <ul style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
          <li>Submit your AFP certificate to DFAT's Consular Services</li>
          <li>DFAT's apostille process takes approximately 5–15 business days (check current wait times at smartraveller.gov.au)</li>
          <li>You can also use a private apostille service for faster turnaround</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div style="padding: 1.5rem; background: var(--white); border-radius: 12px; border-left: 4px solid #3399ff;">
        <h3>Standard Student Visa Documents (All Nationalities)</h3>
        <ul style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
          <li>Completed national visa application form (EX-01)</li>
          <li>Australian passport — valid for at least 1 year beyond course end, minimum 2 blank pages</li>
          <li>2 recent passport photos (white background, EU spec)</li>
          <li>Acceptance/enrolment letter from accredited Spanish institution</li>
          <li>Proof of accommodation in Spain (rental agreement or student housing confirmation)</li>
          <li>Private health insurance valid in Spain for the full duration</li>
          <li>Australian bank statements (3–6 months, liquid funds only)</li>
          <li>Medical certificate from a registered Australian doctor (no communicable diseases)</li>
          <li>Proof of tuition payment or first instalment</li>
          <li>Certified Spanish translation of any documents not already in Spanish</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Application Process ──────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Application Process from Australia</h2>
        <p>Step-by-step guide for Australians applying at the Spanish consulate.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__steps">
        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">1</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Secure Your Enrolment</h3>
            <p>Get a formal acceptance or enrolment letter from your Spanish school or university. Pay at least the first instalment of tuition fees and obtain the receipt.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">2</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Apply for AFP National Police Check</h3>
            <p>Submit your AFP National Police Check application. Allow 2–3 weeks for processing. Once received, submit to DFAT for apostille (allow a further 1–3 weeks).</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">3</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Arrange Health Insurance</h3>
            <p>Obtain private health insurance covering Spain for the full duration of your stay. International student health insurance plans from Australian providers are accepted.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">4</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Compile Your Document Pack</h3>
            <p>Assemble all originals and copies. Have any non-English documents translated by a certified Spanish translator (jurado). Ensure bank statements are recent (within 3 months).</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">5</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Book Your Consulate Appointment</h3>
            <p>Book at the Spanish Consulate in Sydney (busiest) or Melbourne depending on your state. Sydney covers NSW, QLD, and surrounding states. Melbourne covers VIC, SA, WA, TAS, and ACT. Allow 2–4 weeks for an appointment slot.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">6</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Attend, Submit &amp; Wait</h3>
            <p>Attend in person with all documents. Pay the consulate fee. Processing takes 30–60 days. Collect your visa and ensure you depart Australia within the validity window.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Popular Study Destinations ──────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section msv__section--cream">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Popular Destinations for Australian Students</h2>
        <p>Where Australians in Spain tend to study and live.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 1rem;">
        <div style="padding: 1.3rem; background: var(--white); border-radius: 10px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">
          <h3>Barcelona</h3>
          <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--muted); margin: 0.4rem 0 0;">The number-one choice for Australians. Cosmopolitan, beach-adjacent, and home to world-class universities and language schools. Strong expat network makes it easier to settle.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding: 1.3rem; background: var(--white); border-radius: 10px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">
          <h3>Valencia</h3>
          <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--muted); margin: 0.4rem 0 0;">Growing in popularity with Australians. More affordable than Barcelona, excellent quality of life, beautiful city beaches, and a strong language school and university scene.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding: 1.3rem; background: var(--white); border-radius: 10px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">
          <h3>Seville</h3>
          <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--muted); margin: 0.4rem 0 0;">For those seeking full cultural immersion. Outstanding for Spanish language learning, warm climate, vibrant nightlife, and iconic architecture. Very popular with gap year students.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding: 1.3rem; background: var(--white); border-radius: 10px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">
          <h3>Madrid &amp; Granada</h3>
          <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--muted); margin: 0.4rem 0 0;">Madrid suits university degree students and those in business or finance. Granada is beloved for its student atmosphere, low cost of living, and intimate university town feel.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Work Rights ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Work Rights for Australian Student Visa Holders</h2>
        <p>What you can and cannot do during your studies in Spain.</p>
      </div>
      <p>The student visa does not automatically permit work — but <strong>work authorisation</strong> can be applied for after you arrive in Spain. Once approved, student visa holders can work up to <strong>30 hours per week</strong> in most cases (some academic programmes restrict this to 20 hours).</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Apply for work authorisation after registering your TIE (foreigner identity card) in Spain</li>
        <li>Work must be compatible with your studies — you cannot work full-time</li>
        <li>Common jobs: language tutoring, hospitality, retail</li>
        <li>Freelance or self-employment is not permitted under a student visa</li>
        <li>Working Holiday Visa (WHV): Australia has a Youth Mobility agreement with Spain — this is a separate and different visa and not the same as a student visa</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── After Your Visa ──────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section msv__section--cream">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>After Your Student Visa</h2>
        <p>Options for Australians who want to stay in Spain after completing their studies.</p>
      </div>
      <p>Your student visa is renewable for as long as you remain enrolled in an accredited programme. Renewals require evidence of academic progress and updated financial proof.</p>
      <p>The student visa does not convert directly to a work permit, but there are clear pathways:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Job Seeker Visa (Visado Búsqueda de Empleo):</strong> Available to graduates from Spanish universities — 12 months to find work</li>
        <li><strong>Work permit conversion:</strong> If you secure a job offer, you can convert to a work permit directly</li>
        <li><strong>Highly Qualified Professional / EU Blue Card:</strong> For graduates in high-demand sectors</li>
        <li><strong>Spain-Australia Youth Mobility:</strong> Check current agreements if you want flexibility beyond study</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── FAQs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="msv__faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>Do Australian citizens need a student visa for Spain?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. As non-EU nationals, Australians require a Type D student visa for any programme in Spain lasting more than 90 days. For shorter courses (under 90 days), Australians can enter visa-free. The student visa must be obtained at the Spanish Consulate in Sydney or Melbourne before you depart Australia.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which Spanish consulate in Australia handles student visa applications?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Australia has two Spanish consular offices — Sydney and Melbourne. Sydney is the busiest and covers NSW, Queensland, and surrounding states. Melbourne covers Victoria, South Australia, Western Australia, Tasmania, and the ACT. Check the consulate website to confirm which office has jurisdiction for your state.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does my superannuation count as proof of funds?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>No. Superannuation is a locked retirement savings fund and does not count as accessible liquid funds. The Spanish consulate requires evidence of funds you can actually access and use during your studies. You need liquid savings in a regular bank account — typically 3–6 months of statements showing consistent, accessible balances well above the €600/month minimum.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What criminal record check does Australia require?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Australian applicants need an AFP National Police Check, which must be apostilled by DFAT. The AFP check takes approximately 10–15 business days; the DFAT apostille process takes a further 5–15 business days. Start the process at least 2 months before your target consulate appointment to avoid delays.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I do a gap year in Spain on a student visa?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes, if your gap year includes a Spanish language course lasting 90+ days at an accredited school. This is one of the most popular routes for Australians. You cannot, however, enter Spain as a tourist intending to study — the visa must be obtained before you leave Australia. For gap years focused on travel (not study), a tourist entry (visa-free for 90 days) is appropriate.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

